github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/pull/6224#issuecomment-5534600901","viewerDidAuthor":true},{"id":"IC_kwDOUMmgW88AAAABSeNlBQ","author":{"login":"qodo-code-review"},"authorAssociation":"NONE","body":"<h3>Code Review by Qodo</h3>\n\n<code>🐞 Bugs (1)</code>  <code>📘 Rule violations (0)</code>  <code>📜 Skill insights (0)</code>\n\n<img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/light-grey-line.svg\" height=\"10%\" alt=\"Grey Divider\">\n\n<br/>\n\n<img src=\"https://img.shields.io/badge/High-634FD1?style=flat-square\" height=\"20px\" alt=\"Action required\">\n\n<details>\n<summary>  1.  Deletion drops HttpOnly option <code>🐞 Bug</code> <code>≡ Correctness</code></summary>\n\n<br/>\n\n> <details open>\n><summary>Description</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n><b><i>save_session</i></b> no longer forwards <b><i>SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY</i></b> when deleting a session cookie, so\n>Werkzeug uses its <b><i>False</i></b> default and omits <b><i>HttpOnly</i></b> even though Flask defaults the setting to\n><b><i>True</i></b>. This changes the observable <b><i>Set-Cookie</i></b> deletion header and can break consumers or security\n>policies that require the configured cookie attributes consistently.\n></pre>\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Code</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><code>[src/flask/sessions.py[363]](https://github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/pull/6224/files#diff-be4c249245e3745ed5ae394ab2256b76bff507a7eca1aeebdc39b11097c67a8fL363-L363)</code>\n>\n>```diff\n>-                    httponly=httponly,\n>```\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Evidence</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n><pre>\n>Flask retrieves <b><i>SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY</i></b> in <b><i>save_session</i></b>, applies it when creating the session\n>cookie, and defaults it to <b><i>True</i></b>, but the changed deletion call no longer passes that value.\n>Werkzeug documents that <b><i>Response.delete_cookie</i></b> defaults <b><i>httponly</i></b> to <b><i>False</i></b>, proving the\n>omission changes the generated header rather than preserving the configured option.\n></pre>\n>\n> <code>[src/flask/sessions.py[342-346]](https://github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/blob/21648646c577f3eb5e2fd56f1cbd56ebb568556d/src/flask/sessions.py/#L342-L346)</code>\n> <code>[src/flask/sessions.py[356-363]](https://github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/blob/21648646c577f3eb5e2fd56f1cbd56ebb568556d/src/flask/sessions.py/#L356-L363)</code>\n> <code>[src/flask/sessions.py[373-382]](https://github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/blob/21648646c577f3eb5e2fd56f1cbd56ebb568556d/src/flask/sessions.py/#L373-L382)</code>\n> <code>[src/flask/app.py[219-225]](https://github.com/seonho12-54/qodo-coderabbit-benchmark/blob/21648646c577f3eb5e2fd56f1cbd56ebb568556d/src/flask/app.py/#L219-L225)</code>\n> <code>🌐 [Werkzeug documents `Response.delete_cookie(..., httponly=False, ...)`, so omitting the argument removes HttpOnly from the deletion header.](https://werkzeug.palletsprojects.com/en/stable/wrappers/)</code>\n></details>\n\n> <details>\n><summary>Agent prompt</summary>\n><br/>\n>\n>```\n>The issue below was found during a code review. Follow the provided context and guidance below and implement a solution\n>\n>## Issue description\n>Session cookie deletion no longer forwards Flask's configured `SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY` value, causing deletion headers to silently use Werkzeug's `False` default.\n>\n>## Issue Context\n>`save_session` already retrieves the setting as `httponly` and uses it when setting the cookie. Preserve the same option when calling `response.delete_cookie`, and add coverage for deletion with `SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY=True`.\n>\n>## Fix Focus Areas\n>- src/flask/sessions.py[342-363]\n>- tests/test_basic.py[313-353]\n>```\n> <code>ⓘ Copy this prompt and use it to remediate the issue with your preferred AI generation tools</code>\n></details>\n\n<hr/>\n</details>\n\n\n\n<img src=\"https://www.qodo.ai/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/lig